From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 07:40:29 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: pmp: take the PMP and its report through a system
 sleep

Two traced macOS sleeps on T8140 show the same thirteen mailbox
messages. About 61 ms before the commit the AP state then the IOP state
go to 0x201, each acknowledged. About 37 ms after the wake the firmware
sends HELLO unasked, then its endpoint map, reports IOP state 0x20
unasked, and takes AP state 0x20.

The idle is a point of no return: an idled PMP doesn't come back with
its power kept. So where the sleep controller can take it, the idle and
the stop run in the sleep's last step, after every refusal, with
interrupts off: the PMP registers there once, readies itself in
suspend_late, and a refused sleep finds it still running. Elsewhere it
idles in suspend_late, after the consumers' suspend and any NVMe stop.
It comes back in resume_early, before the devices it serves resume,
through the shared RTKit return. Nothing the firmware asked for at probe
is repeated. As macOS does, after the idle pair the CPU is stopped and
the firmware's SRAM range (0x3c000 to 0x98000 on T8140, match data) is
copied out; after the wake it's written back before the run bit. The
copy's memory is taken before anything is asked of the firmware, so
running out refuses the sleep cleanly. A sleep refused after the stop
leaves the firmware stopped, since a restart with the power kept can't
work, and the next suspend finds it stopped and goes on.

The report: entries' power requests reach the PMP through it, and each
waits for an acknowledgment that a stopped PMP can't give. So requests
are withdrawn just before the PMP stops, as macOS's request word reads 0
by then, and held in the books until the firmware is back. After the
wake it's handed everything again, with the request word from the
sleep's start for the bits no entry owns, since the report's SRAM loses
power. A driver that switches its device's power itself around a sleep
can set its one entry's request (macOS does that for the GPU, clearing
its bit before lowering GFX and setting it after raising it). A power
command during the sleep gets -EAGAIN and doesn't latch a failure.

The GPU's stop moves to the commit as well, and withdraws the GPU's
request there, while the PMP still runs. So an entry's driver can mark
it as withdrawn at the commit: apple_pmp_report_sleep() leaves it set
when the PMP stops at the commit too, and
apple_pmp_report_entry_withdraw_polled() withdraws it with interrupts
off, past the hold on requests, polling for the PMP's acknowledgment.

On T8140 the PMP offers no sysfs unbind, as on the retained-vote J713:
an unbind leaves its firmware running, and a commit with the PMP running
reset the SoC, so its stale sleep registration would refuse every sleep
until reboot.

Signed-off-by: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>

[j604 backport: native port on our report: request books (known/shadow/commit_owned/defer/sleep_request) on our offsets, no DVFS shadow, ready work, DRAM attributes or ANE window; set_state keeps the books and holds while the PMP sleeps; T8132 sram_backup None (TO-VERIFY: SRAM state range not in the J604 ADT); on J604 apple_pmp_display_retained_board() is true, so suspend_late refuses a firmware sleep with -EBUSY while the PMP is bound (CODE)]
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5

